// src/lsp/lsp_colour.zen
// `textDocument/semanticTokens/full`, lexical form — the colours, from
// the compiler's own lexer.
//
// NAMED FOR WHAT IT PRODUCES AND NOT FOR THE REQUEST, because the
// request's name is a lie about this file: nothing here is semantic.
// `semanticTokens` is the only route the protocol offers for colour and
// it is the one this server answers, but every answer below comes from
// `scan` and a `TokenKind`, with no `Checker`, no build and no import
// resolved. That is the whole point — see the header of `editors/README.md`
// for the argument this file is the other half of.
//
// WHY THIS AND NOT A TEXTMATE GRAMMAR. `docs/PLAN.md:137` names a second
// grammar as the failure the plan exists to prevent, and a generated one
// would be a third ungated artifact. `docs/design_lsp.md` §2 named the
// route that costs neither: the token stream is already here, so the
// colours come from the lexer the compiler itself runs and CANNOT
// disagree with it about what a token is.
//
// WHAT IT COSTS TO ANSWER: one `scan`. No `Parser`, no `Checker`, no
// `Build`, nothing read from any disk. So unlike hover and diagnostics
// this answers on an unsaved buffer with no workspace, in a file that
// does not parse, and in about the time it takes to read the bytes.
// A file with lexical faults still has tokens (`src/lex/lex.zen`'s
// contract) and every one of them is still coloured.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE FIVE THINGS A SEMANTIC-TOKENS SERVER GETS WRONG, and what this
// file does about each.
//
// 1. THE ENCODING IS DELTAS AND THE DELTAS ARE FROM THE PREVIOUS TOKEN.
//    Five integers per token — `deltaLine, deltaStartChar, length,
//    tokenType, tokenModifier` — where `deltaStartChar` is relative to
//    the previous token's start ONLY when the two share a line, and
//    absolute otherwise. `Painter` below is those two numbers and
//    nothing else. Getting it wrong tints a file that drifts further
//    off the further down you read, which looks like a rendering bug
//    and is arithmetic.
//
// 2. THE NUMBERS ARE UTF-16, ON BOTH AXES THAT HAVE ONE. `character`
//    and `length` are UTF-16 code units into the line, not bytes and
//    not codepoints, and `lsp_pos.zen` is the only file that converts.
//    A 4-byte emoji is 2 units and 4 bytes and an `é` is 1 unit and 2
//    bytes, so a length taken from `end.offset - start.offset` is
//    right for exactly the ASCII half of the world.
//
// 3. A MULTI-LINE TOKEN IS SPLIT, ONE RUN PER LINE. The standard
//    encoding cannot express a token that crosses a newline unless the
//    client advertised `multilineTokenSupport`, which most do not and
//    which this server does not ask about. SPLITTING IS CHOSEN OVER
//    OMITTING: a block comment is the one token here that spans lines,
//    it is also the one a reader most wants greyed out, and an omitted
//    one leaves a hole in the middle of a file. Each run gets the same
//    colour, and a run of zero length — a blank line inside a block
//    comment — is not emitted at all, because a zero-width token
//    colours nothing and every client is entitled to be confused by one.
//
// 4. THE LEGEND IS DECLARED IN `initialize` AND INDEXED HERE.
//    `tokenType` is an INDEX into the `tokenTypes` array the server
//    advertised, so `index_of` and `write_legend` are one fact said
//    twice and must agree. They are both below, in the same order, in
//    one file, and the corpus test prints both halves.
//
// 5. `Ident` IS ONE KIND AND COLOUR WANTS SEVERAL — AND THIS FILE DOES
//    NOT GUESS. A lexer cannot tell a type from a function from a
//    variable: `Vec` and `add` and `n` are the same token, and Zen makes
//    that sharper than most languages because a type is a bare
//    identifier (`DESIGN.md`) with no keyword in front of it. Colouring
//    by capitalisation would be a SECOND, WRONG specification of what a
//    name means, living in `src/lsp/` where nothing may specify the
//    language. So every `Ident` is `variable`, uniformly, and telling
//    them apart is `design_lsp.md` §2's semantic row at L4 — it needs
//    `defs_of` per identifier and a memo on `Ident`, which is a change
//    in `src/sema/`. Hover already refuses to print sema's poison for
//    the same reason: a wrong answer is worse than none.
//
// DELIMITERS ARE NOT COLOURED, and that is the same refusal one step
// down. `(`, `)`, `[`, `]`, `{`, `}`, `,`, `;`, `.` and `:` are
// structure, not meaning; a theme's default foreground is what a reader
// expects them in, and calling a brace an `operator` would be this file
// deciding something the lexer did not say. Operators proper — the
// arithmetic, comparison, logical and binding glyphs — are `operator`,
// because that is what they are.
//
// EVERY VARIANT OF `TokenKind` IS LISTED IN `colour_of`, with no `_`
// arm. A token shape added to `src/lex/` should make this file fail to
// compile and force a decision, rather than quietly arriving uncoloured.

str, String = std.text
Alloc, AllocError = std.mem
Range = std.core
scan, Source, Token, TokenKind = lex
write_decimal = lsp.lsp_frame
// `written()` is `Ok(())` with the writer's error type already on it, so
// a match arm that writes nothing sits beside one that writes something
// without the two having different types.
written = lsp.lsp_json
wire_at, units_of = lsp.lsp_pos

// The legend, in order. A `tokenType` on the wire is a position in this
// list, so the ORDER BELOW IS THE WIRE FORMAT and the two functions
// under it are how it is spelled and how it is counted.
//
// `Text` is the legend's `string`. It is spelled differently only
// because `TokenKind.Str` is matched in the same file and one name for
// two things in one match is how a reader is misled.
//
// SIX AND NOT MORE. The protocol's standard type names are a long list
// and every one this server cannot honestly produce is left out —
// `type`, `function`, `parameter`, `property` are exactly the ones item
// 5 of the header refuses. A legend that advertises them and never
// emits them tells a theme to prepare colours it will never see.
Colour* = Keyword | Text | Number | Comment | Operator | Variable

// A colour's index in the legend. THE NUMBERS THE WIRE CARRIES.
index_of* = (colour: Colour) usize {
    colour.match({
        Keyword  => 0,
        Text     => 1,
        Number   => 2,
        Comment  => 3,
        Operator => 4,
        Variable => 5,
    })
}

// A colour's name in the legend. STANDARD LSP TOKEN TYPE NAMES, all six,
// so any theme colours them with no configuration — a server inventing
// `zenKeyword` would be a server whose output is grey everywhere until
// the user writes a `semanticTokenColorCustomizations` block.
name_of* = (colour: Colour) str {
    colour.match({
        Keyword  => "keyword",
        Text     => "string",
        Number   => "number",
        Comment  => "comment",
        Operator => "operator",
        Variable => "variable",
    })
}

// The legend as `initialize` advertises it, written by walking the
// variants IN INDEX ORDER — so the order lives in exactly one place a
// reader can check against `index_of` by reading down the page.
//
// NO MODIFIERS. `tokenModifiers` is empty and every token this file
// emits carries a modifier set of 0. `declaration`, `readonly`,
// `static` and the rest are all facts about what a name MEANS, which is
// item 5 again: a lexer knows none of them, and an empty list is how the
// protocol spells "there are none".
write_legend* = (out :: String) Res<(), AllocError> {
    out.add_bytes("{\"tokenTypes\":[\"").try();
    out.add_bytes(name_of(Colour.Keyword)).try();
    next_name(Colour.Text, out).try();
    next_name(Colour.Number, out).try();
    next_name(Colour.Comment, out).try();
    next_name(Colour.Operator, out).try();
    next_name(Colour.Variable, out).try();
    out.add_bytes("\"],\"tokenModifiers\":[]}")
}

next_name = (colour: Colour, out :: String) Res<(), AllocError> {
    out.add_bytes("\",\"").try();
    out.add_bytes(name_of(colour))
}

// WHAT EACH TOKEN SHAPE IS COLOURED, and `None` for the shapes this
// file will not colour. Every variant of `TokenKind` appears, in the
// order `src/lex/lex_token.zen` declares them, so the two can be read
// side by side.
//
// `Ident` IS `variable` AND THAT IS THE HONEST ANSWER, not the useful
// one — see item 5 of the header. `Char` is `string` because the
// protocol's legend has no character type and a character literal is a
// literal of text; naming it `number` because it holds one byte would
// be a guess about what the author meant by it.
//
// `Eof` SPANS NOTHING, by construction, and a zero-length token is not
// something to hand a client even where the encoding permits it.
colour_of* = (kind: TokenKind) Res<Colour> {
    kind.match({
        Ident        => Ok(Colour.Variable),
        Int(_)       => Ok(Colour.Number),
        Float        => Ok(Colour.Number),
        Str          => Ok(Colour.Text),
        Char         => Ok(Colour.Text),
        True         => Ok(Colour.Keyword),
        False        => Ok(Colour.Keyword),
        Consume      => Ok(Colour.Keyword),
        AtSelf       => Ok(Colour.Keyword),
        AtMeta       => Ok(Colour.Keyword),
        AtScope      => Ok(Colour.Keyword),
        LineComment  => Ok(Colour.Comment),
        BlockComment => Ok(Colour.Comment),
        ParenOpen    => None,
        ParenClose   => None,
        BracketOpen  => None,
        BracketClose => None,
        BraceOpen    => None,
        BraceClose   => None,
        Comma        => None,
        Semicolon    => None,
        Dot          => None,
        Ellipsis     => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        Colon        => None,
        ColonColon   => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        ColonColonEq => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        Eq           => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        EqEq         => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        Arrow        => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        Bang         => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        BangEq       => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        Lt           => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        LtEq         => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        Gt           => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        GtEq         => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        Amp          => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        AmpAmp       => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        Bar          => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        BarBar       => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        Plus         => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        PlusWrap     => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        Minus        => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        MinusWrap    => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        Star         => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        StarWrap     => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        Slash        => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        Percent      => Ok(Colour.Operator),
        Eof          => None,
    })
}

// --- the encoding ------------------------------------------------------

// WHERE THE PREVIOUS TOKEN STARTED, which is the entire state the
// encoding needs, plus whether a comma is owed.
//
// It begins at line 0, character 0 rather than at "nothing yet", and
// that is not a shortcut: the protocol says the first token's deltas are
// measured from the origin, so the same subtraction that handles every
// later token handles the first one and there is no special case to get
// wrong.
Painter = {
    line :: usize,
    character :: usize,
    said :: bool,

    // One run, as five integers. `length` is already in UTF-16 units and
    // `colour` is already an index — both conversions happen before a
    // number reaches this method, so nothing here can convert one twice.
    put = (self :: @Self, line: usize, character: usize, length: usize,
           colour: Colour, out :: String) Res<(), AllocError> {
        self.said.match({ true => out.add_bytes(","), false => written() }).try();
        delta_line = line - self.line;
        // THE ONE BRANCH IN THE WHOLE ENCODING: a token on the same line
        // as its predecessor measures from that predecessor's start; one
        // on a later line measures from the start of its own line.
        delta_character = (delta_line == 0).match({
            true  => character - self.character,
            false => character,
        });
        write_decimal(delta_line, out).try();
        out.add_bytes(",").try();
        write_decimal(delta_character, out).try();
        out.add_bytes(",").try();
        write_decimal(length, out).try();
        out.add_bytes(",").try();
        write_decimal(index_of(colour), out).try();
        // The modifier set, always empty — see `write_legend`.
        out.add_bytes(",0").try();
        self.line = line;
        self.character = character;
        self.said = true;
        Ok(());
    }

    // A token, as one run per line it covers.
    //
    // THE FIRST RUN'S CHARACTER IS CONVERTED AND EVERY LATER ONE IS
    // ZERO. A run that begins because a newline ended the last one
    // begins at the first byte of its line, which is character 0 in
    // every encoding there is — so the conversion is paid once per
    // token and not once per line, and the two cannot disagree.
    paint = (self :: @Self, text: str, token: Token, colour: Colour,
             out :: String) Res<(), AllocError> {
        stop = token.span.end.offset;
        from ::= token.span.start.offset;
        line ::= zero_based(token.span.start.line);
        character ::= wire_at(text, token.span.start.line,
                              token.span.start.col).character;
        loop(() { from < stop }, (h) {
            upto = break_in(text, from, stop);
            self.run(text, line, character, from, upto, colour, out).try();
            // Past the `\n`, which belongs to neither run: it is inside
            // the token's bytes and it colours nothing.
            from = upto + 1;
            line = line + 1;
            character = 0;
        });
        Ok(());
    }

    // One line's worth, unless it is empty. A blank line inside a block
    // comment is a real run of zero units, and zero-width tokens are not
    // worth the client's trouble.
    run = (self :: @Self, text: str, line: usize, character: usize,
           from: usize, upto: usize, colour: Colour, out :: String)
          Res<(), AllocError> {
        (upto > from).match({
            true  => self.put(line, character, units_of(text, from, upto),
                              colour, out),
            false => written(),
        })
    }
}

// The first `\n` in `from..stop`, or `stop`. What splits a multi-line
// token, and the only place this file looks at a byte.
break_in = (text: str, from: usize, stop: usize) usize {
    Range(from, stop).find((i) { text.index(i) == '\n' }).match({
        Ok(i) => i,
        None  => stop,
    })
}

// --- the answer --------------------------------------------------------

// `text` coloured, as the protocol's reply body: `{"data":[..]}`.
//
// `name` is what the document is called for the purpose of a `Source`.
// It is the CLIENT'S URI verbatim, exactly as `hover_at` uses it, and it
// reaches nothing outside this call — a lexical pass has no second file
// to name.
//
// A LEX FAULT IS NOT REPORTED HERE AND NOT ALLOWED TO STOP ANYTHING.
// `scan` hands back every token it could read together with every fault
// it found, `lsp_diag.zen` is what says the faults out loud, and a
// buffer mid-keystroke is the normal case rather than the exception —
// a server that went grey the moment a quote was opened would be worse
// than one with no colour at all.
write_tokens* = (a: Alloc, name: str, text: str, out :: String)
                Res<(), AllocError> {
    lexed = scan(a, Source(file: name, text: text)).try();
    painter ::= Painter(line: 0, character: 0, said: false);
    out.add_bytes("{\"data\":[").try();
    lexed.tokens.loop((h, token) {
        colour_of(token.kind).match({
            None    => written(),
            Ok(one) => painter.paint(text, token, one, out),
        }).try();
    });
    out.add_bytes("]}")
}

// A 1-based line as the wire's 0-based one. Line 0 cannot come out of
// the lexer — `lex_token.zen` says lines are 1-based from the cursor up
// — but a subtraction on a `usize` traps rather than going negative, so
// the impossible input clamps here for the same reason `lsp_pos.zen`
// clamps it there: a server that traps on a number is a server that
// stops answering.
zero_based = (line: usize) usize {
    (line == 0).match({ true => 0, false => line - 1 })
}
